    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: which level of government, and which of several competing public broadcasters, is actually telling this particular story. The weekly rueda de prensa and the no-questions controversy are the two faces of the same core ritual, one a genuine working institution and one a contested departure from it that has become a story in its own right. RTVE's governance history and the comunidades autónomas' own separate public broadcasters are the two structural facts that make Spain's public-broadcasting landscape considerably more fragmented, and considerably more politically contested, than the more balance-regulated systems covered elsewhere in this course. Tertulias and WhatsApp are the two channels, one public and combative, one private and leak-prone, where the actual texture of Spanish political communication plays out day to day, underneath the more formal rueda de prensa ritual. Digital press polarization is the landscape that message ultimately lands in, the transparency law is the proactive obligation increasingly shaping what a communications office must disclose before anyone even asks, and the cordón sanitario debate is the recurring test of how far mainstream media and politics are willing to go in managing a rapidly growing party's own access to all of the above. Social media is the newest layer, increasingly bypassing the rueda de prensa that this lecture began with entirely. As you follow Spanish political communication from here forward, ask which broadcaster, which region, and which platform is actually shaping the version of the story you are reading, and you will generally find Spain's own genuinely fragmented, multi-level media landscape explains considerably more of it than any single office's own intended message ever could on its own.",
